                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "7ea834a5-85ce-33dd-b3ba-f9b86a07bd92",
                        "entry_id": "3dfd7a92-0d61-377e-a56a-65358c5bd5ff",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A boor, lout."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "77951817-e624-481f-8e57-c0b54b9cb209",
                                "sense_id": "7ea834a5-85ce-33dd-b3ba-f9b86a07bd92",
                                "text": "Sham’st thou not coistrel, loathsome dunghill swad."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a1c72aae-b85b-43be-a6f7-9246afa62148",
                                "sense_id": "7ea834a5-85ce-33dd-b3ba-f9b86a07bd92",
                                "text": "There was one busy fellow was their leader, / A blunt, squat swad, but lower than yourself."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "249bb8a7-934c-41cf-97ec-e4b5e5462efd",
                                "sense_id": "7ea834a5-85ce-33dd-b3ba-f9b86a07bd92",
                                "text": "Country swains, and silly swads."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "41243662-e930-3eda-a5f8-bee98cd4afd8",
                        "entry_id": "3dfd7a92-0d61-377e-a56a-65358c5bd5ff",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A thin layer of refuse at the bottom of a seam."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "045d4481-542a-30ff-82e1-9e1de22046b9",
                        "entry_id": "3dfd7a92-0d61-377e-a56a-65358c5bd5ff",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A cod, or pod, as of beans or peas."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Northern",
                            "UK",
                            "dialectal",
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "2ffce38c-c422-4184-b53a-0bb0daa727db",
                                "sense_id": "045d4481-542a-30ff-82e1-9e1de22046b9",
                                "text": "They must bee gathered in the decrease of the moone, presently vpon their being ripe, for else they drie vp and fall out of their swads:"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e4658658-8b67-4560-bdaf-a1f08add88ea",
                                "sense_id": "045d4481-542a-30ff-82e1-9e1de22046b9",
                                "text": "... the Bean is not seen till first it be unhusk'd, and that its swad or hull be shaled, and pilled from off it : […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ea0ac126-d587-4096-a162-f6d885411e9c",
                                "sense_id": "045d4481-542a-30ff-82e1-9e1de22046b9",
                                "text": "Swad, in the north, is a peascod shell — thence used for an empty, shallow-headed fellow."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "bc68ec64-39f1-4317-bc7f-940f712771ef",
                                "sense_id": "045d4481-542a-30ff-82e1-9e1de22046b9",
                                "text": "... it is the stem and leaf that is wanted , more than the swad or grain."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b7ec1bae-d113-4dd1-aff2-661d924929f0",
                                "sense_id": "045d4481-542a-30ff-82e1-9e1de22046b9",
                                "text": "It is a fruit growing on a tree, a species of acacia. \"The pods hang down, and only the swad is used for feeding cattle.\" Why , then , does the Chancellor of the Exchequer tax locust beans as a kind of wheat? Have his Park-lane friends warned him against manna?"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
